  "query_preview": "..The mid/upper-level trough with a closed low at its\nbase is starting to push into Arizona as tropical moisture \ncontinues to sit across northern Arizona. Meanwhile, Hurricane \nPolo is moving across Baja California. Radar shows heavier \nshowers and thunderstorms are developing across eastern AZ, and \nthis will be pushing W to NW across the State today as the trough \nand closed low move eastward today. This will support impactful \nweather across northern AZ today, with a greater focus over \neastern AZ this evening and Wednesday.\n\nFrom this morning through Wednesday morning, the low is forecast \nto move across northern Arizona. As it does so, additional \natmospheric moisture from Hurricane Polo will be entrained into \nthe low. With increased synoptic-scale forcing associated with the\npassing low, greater storm activity and higher rainfall rates are\nforecast. High-resolution model guidance continue to highlight \nthe eastern Mogollon Rim and White Mountains as the area with the \nhighest flood risk with 24 hour rainfall totals up to 3 inches. Another\nsignature of an inch or greater is starting to appear in the 3-hr\nQPF HREF guidance near the UT and AZ border, near Lake Powell, \nthis afternoon and early evening hours so this will be a trend to \nwatch. We will also see higher 0-6km Bulk Wind Shear, with values \nranging from 40-60 kts. Despite the strong shear, forecast CAPE \nvalues at or below 600 J/kg may limit the overall severe \nthunderstorm threat, though isolated severe storms cannot be ruled\nout given the benefit of forcing from the low. So, all things \nconsidered, we are looking at a moderate/high risk of \nflooding/flash flooding, with a lower risk of strong storms \nproducing large hail and severe wind gusts. A Flood Watch remains \nin effect for northern Arizona through Tuesday evening.\n\nOn Wednesday, the low center moves into New Mexico, with chances\nfor showers and storms primarily remaining across eastern \nArizona. High pressure is forecast to bring a return of dry \nweather, with daytime temperatures warming to near average on \nThursday and into the weekend.",
